### auth_lab.http — Authentication Gauntlet Lab
### Write your REST Client request blocks under each phase header.
### Run a block with Ctrl+Alt+R (Windows/Linux) or Cmd+Alt+R (macOS).
### Hint: all endpoints live under http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 1 — Basic Authentication
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (unauthenticated)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1

###

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (with credentials)
# REST Client encodes username:password in Base64 automatically
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=

###

### Phase 1 — Replay Attack (Synthesis Challenge)
# An attacker who intercepts the raw Authorization header line off the network
# can simply paste it directly into their own HTTP request header:
# Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=
# This replay attack works because Basic Authentication transmits static, unchanged credentials with every request.
# Base64 is merely an encoding scheme (obfuscation), NOT encryption. The attacker does not even need to decode
# the header to obtain the original password; replaying the raw encoded string grants full access.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=

